faq:
  - q: "What is a panel interview?"
    a: "A panel interview is a format where 2–5 interviewers evaluate a single candidate simultaneously. Each panelist typically focuses on a different dimension: technical depth, behavioral history, culture fit, domain expertise, or cross-functional alignment. Panel interviews are common at mid-to-senior levels, in final-round loops, and in organizations that require multi-stakeholder buy-in for hiring decisions."
  - q: "How do you manage eye contact in a panel interview?"
    a: "Start your answer making eye contact with whoever asked the question. As you deliver the body of your answer, sweep the panel — spending 2–3 seconds with each panelist. Return to the questioner to close your answer. Do not stare at one person for a long answer — it leaves other panelists disengaged. Do not try to split attention perfectly mid-sentence — it looks scattered."
  - q: "How do you handle multiple questions at once in a panel interview?"
    a: "If two interviewers ask different questions simultaneously, pause, acknowledge both, and address each in sequence. 'I want to make sure I address both questions — [first person's name], let me start with yours, then I'll come back to [second person's name].' This signals organizational clarity and manages the situation professionally rather than awkwardly picking one and ignoring the other."
  - q: "How should you prepare for a panel interview?"
    a: "Research each panelist on LinkedIn before the interview. Understand their role and likely evaluation focus — an engineering manager will probe differently than a product lead or an HR partner. Prepare STAR stories that can speak to multiple dimensions simultaneously. Prepare specific questions for each panelist based on their function. Confirm who will be on the panel with your recruiter contact."
  - q: "Is a panel interview harder than a one-on-one interview?"
    a: "It is more demanding — you are managing multiple relationships simultaneously and cannot rely on building rapport with a single person. But it is also more forgiving in one way: a weak answer with one panelist can be offset by a strong answer with another. The key is to stay calibrated across the full room, not to win over one person at the expense of others."
